update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('ca91a43a-73c7-4337-b1b4-5d65a85fb0cd'::uuid,'9d8209771525c9fa4ed556912da13234','Both speakers accept this, so it can''t be the disagreement. Antonia says the market "is often volatile" but "provides the opportunity to make a large profit quickly." Maria opens with "I agree" on the quick profits, and "just as likely to take a huge loss" only adds to the volatility. Two people on the same side of a claim aren''t disagreeing about it.'),
('3c0aa30a-5d94-452e-9e32-64f25c50bf96'::uuid,'0c7c5deb5e55b03374b5f792d411c25f','Neither speaker says savings bonds give a large return. Maria praises bonds for a "constant, reliable income," which is about steadiness, not size. Antonia never mentions bonds at all. With no claim about the size of the return from either speaker, this can''t be the point they disagree on.'),
('80308a7d-1bc1-41f2-a2e9-5ac296b36dbf'::uuid,'8c12697b64e27fc4e7c647a1dbd79976','This is the tempting one, because Maria clearly agrees: stocks risk a huge loss, and bonds pay a constant, reliable income. Now test Antonia. Does Antonia think the stock market is safer? No. Antonia admits the market "is often volatile" and calls it the best place to invest anyway. Best and safest are different things. Antonia could agree that bonds are safer and still prefer stocks for the upside. Only one speaker takes a side here.'),
('00fdd75c-6f92-4738-b116-ea54f1fe6684'::uuid,'3b428c736a3403acf1b47b6a6ab25781','Run both tests. Maria AGREES: Maria grants the quick profits but chooses savings bonds for their "constant, reliable income," because the market carries an equal chance of a huge loss. That''s picking reliable income over the riskier shot at a big, fast profit. Antonia DISAGREES: Antonia admits the volatility and still calls the stock market "the best place to invest your money" because of the chance at a large, quick profit. That''s picking the riskier opportunity.

Antonia never says "reliable income," and you don''t need the exact words. When both speakers grant the same facts, look for the disagreement in what each one does with them. Here they weigh the same tradeoff and land on opposite sides.'),
('cb3f070d-412c-49c6-bc57-107b5491fddb'::uuid,'11bb6401551203beee9f916f2180b77f','Test both positions. Antonio values occasional great chances: never taking them means one "misses the opportunities." Marla doesn''t object. Marla says a moderate life must sometimes risk going too far, so Marla seems to value the occasional chance too. Both lean the same way, so there''s no disagreement here.'),
('f66bca41-4b1b-4cd5-a668-68df25932c3d'::uuid,'0cbfe9e8130558bce201ec33a3f51dcc','This names the definitional clash. Antonio says never deviating from the middle course is enough for a life of moderation. Marla says someone who never risks going too far is "failing to live a life of moderation." So they give different answers to what moderation requires: for Antonio, nothing more than staying in the middle; for Marla, occasionally leaving it.

The answer is abstract, so translate it back. What does a life of moderation require? For these two speakers, that becomes: is never deviating enough? Antonio says yes, Marla says no. When an answer is general, plug each speaker''s specific claim into it and check that you get one yes and one no.'),
('93bfb01f-501a-4983-9028-53a5635d57ac'::uuid,'b5b08cf28f1376d039999da10a819a37','Neither speaker mentions other virtues. Antonio talks about spontaneity and opportunity as things that never deviating costs, but never calls them virtues or asks whether they can sit alongside moderation. Marla only talks about moderation itself. With no position from either side, this can''t be the disagreement.'),
('415cc118-5333-43af-af8d-e2e49d451f1b'::uuid,'675880a0e19ba285592cd7521e26b635','This sounds close, because both speakers talk about leaving the middle course. But neither gives a frequency or says how often a person ought to do it. Antonio only says that people who "occasionally" take great chances gain opportunities, and Marla only says moderation must itself be moderate. Both point toward sometimes, which puts them on the same side, not opposite ones.'),
('2ea891a1-eb77-4526-b64e-1ed34a854950'::uuid,'697d354235cd45ea6233e856734b58df','Marla would likely agree, since being "moderate even in one''s moderation" suggests a little spontaneity is healthy. But Antonio doesn''t disagree. Antonio counts the lost "joy of spontaneity" as a cost of never deviating, which suggests Antonio values spontaneity too. Two speakers who lean the same way aren''t disagreeing.'),
('264725cb-e3ab-4cf9-9b8c-9e002609c963'::uuid,'5e78e7f83167c3fa584122538ec35470','Priscilla does compare skill, but inside science fiction: its writers handle new technologies more skillfully than new social arrangements. Nobody compares conventional fiction writers with science fiction writers, and Lutsina never mentions skill at all. With neither speaker taking a side, this can''t be the disagreement.'),
('b15dece1-1bf2-4de9-83bc-ab861887f325'::uuid,'14369ce2d6b9eacb78d70fcdc51e3fc7','Lutsina AGREES: science fiction "has the potential to be a richer source of social criticism than is conventional fiction." "More promise" is another way to say Lutsina''s "potential to be a richer source." Priscilla DISAGREES: "the most effective social criticism results from faithfully presenting the current social realities," which is what conventional fiction does. Priscilla puts conventional fiction on top.

The move: find each speaker''s conclusion and ask whether they answer the same question. When two conclusions rank the same two things in opposite order, look for an answer that restates that ranking in new words.'),
('171dfb5e-e82b-4267-8bb2-9e438d2b4fcf'::uuid,'ba1626dd4f178e41e534485f46cb7dee','Priscilla probably agrees, since Priscilla treats the gap between technologies and social arrangements as a "limitation." But Lutsina never mentions technology. Lutsina only says science fiction writers can envisage new social arrangements, which says nothing about whether favoring technology is a weakness. One speaker with a view isn''t enough; a disagreement needs both.'),
('62d11c09-7ae9-462c-86c2-6fd324aa47ac'::uuid,'1fb5db3fc0a14aa9dd1695daa246fd3a','This is the trap. Lutsina would likely disagree with it, because Lutsina thinks imagining radically new arrangements makes science fiction a richer source of criticism. But does Priscilla say such criticism is "not effective"? No. Priscilla says the most effective criticism comes from presenting current realities. Calling one approach the most effective doesn''t make the other ineffective. Second place isn''t zero. Without Priscilla on the agree side, there''s no disagreement.'),
('23c2f278-4af7-4e3c-b15c-8e1d31e197a2'::uuid,'87da74beed70e84459cba6147dae15f2','"Best" is a ranking, and neither speaker ranks chess against other activities. Graham uses chess as one example supporting a broad claim about activities governed by fixed principles. Adelaide only talks about how the chess win happened. With no position from either speaker, this can''t be the disagreement.'),
('f0b601f8-a1ad-4d2c-8fda-0b24c1d47fc9'::uuid,'e1a8ffb4dc7dd9db4145c276a1f17a84','Neither speaker says whether chess is typical of what humans usually do. Graham only needs chess to be an activity "governed by fixed principles," not a typical one. Adelaide talks about the programmers'' work, not about how chess compares with everyday human thinking. No positions, no disagreement.'),
('bd8b3976-1153-4505-bd61-b01c60bd8c8b'::uuid,'ab1488bcaaf26428ff5a3f35f9114708','Graham AGREES: the computer''s win "shows" that machines can master fixed-principle activities, so Graham counts the win as the machine''s accomplishment. Adelaide DISAGREES: the computer was "simply an extension of the people who programmed it," and the programmers defeated the champion "using a computer." The credit goes to the people.

Adelaide''s reply opens with "you are overlooking." That tells you Adelaide is attacking how Graham reads the evidence, not the conclusion directly. So look for the disagreement in the evidence itself: who actually beat the champion.'),
('379db44e-a024-4912-b720-af13ef49f92a'::uuid,'02dde599846689050fda9d60c5627739','Graham might accept this, since Graham moves from mastering fixed-principle activities to a "truly intelligent machine." But Adelaide doesn''t take the other side. Adelaide never says that performing by fixed principles fails to show intelligence, only that the intelligence behind this win belonged to the programmers. Adelaide could accept D and still credit the people.'),
('f3541d8a-33eb-407d-85e2-562b1e58d1d6'::uuid,'070b67213efbcce33809afb7a094dc24','Graham says machines can master these activities, which goes further than aiding them, so Graham would likely accept that tools can help. Adelaide treats the computer as an extension of its programmers, basically a tool, but says nothing about "any" fixed-principle activity. Neither speaker denies this, so there''s no disagreement.'),
('129a49b3-bfdf-4aa7-a83c-5f5720b45195'::uuid,'b2c1dae622c8aa73fce1527c27203668','Tate AGREES: material needs matter more than freedom and autonomy, and some countries meet those needs better as autocracies. Put those together and those countries are better off as autocracies. Wong DISAGREES: "all countries are better off as democracies." One exception is enough to contradict an "all" claim, and A asserts exactly that exception.

When one speaker makes an "all" claim, check whether the other speaker supplies a counterexample. That''s often the cleanest disagreement on the page.'),
('17499598-763f-4132-9589-4c6c984dae87'::uuid,'eed32fd5f227d3195154a2695e0c6895','Tate disagrees: the "simple material needs of people are more important" than freedom and autonomy. But Wong never ranks freedom and autonomy against anything. Wong says countries are better off as democracies without saying why, so we can''t put Wong on the agree side. An answer only one speaker addresses can''t be their disagreement.'),
('bebfb802-e001-40c5-be87-2afd037cd852'::uuid,'5bf35a53b6c059d936e2b5c9710e5873','Tempting, because Wong mentions countries that aren''t democratic yet. But Wong says a transitional stage is required "before a country can become democratic," which describes a delay, not an impossibility. Wong never says some countries can''t make it. Tate doesn''t address whether countries can become democracies at all. No positions, no disagreement.'),
('b3cb96cf-5c96-44c9-ad5c-16ac358f867e'::uuid,'64dedafeae95b2875c2fb5ed0917e344','Tate says this word for word: freedom and autonomy "are of genuine value." Wong doesn''t object. If anything, someone who thinks every country is better off as a democracy likely values what democracy provides. Agreement, or at most silence, isn''t a disagreement.'),
('c0cf5f4d-61e8-4033-9956-1eab3bbac7f4'::uuid,'08fd09f3ecc2f57c7f80af01cb6db64e','Tate says some countries "can better meet these needs as autocracies," which is a comparison. A democracy can meet those needs and still meet them less well than an autocracy, so Tate doesn''t have to deny this. And Wong never talks about material needs. Neither speaker takes a clear position.'),
('e6e3674d-1b5b-4e8a-bab7-82c680153aa8'::uuid,'e558e6b16eb7ad8f38a66bb91a7be115','Ana says it is not the government''s business to prevent people from doing things that harm only themselves. Pankaj never says what the government''s business is; Pankaj only describes where the ban applies. Pankaj could fully accept Ana''s principle and still object, because the objection is about whether the ban falls under it.'),
('0ac01bec-dc9d-45df-bbe7-cc2615ebf4af'::uuid,'de3acb1c80c9ce5f404bdba615ad67f8','Ana appeals to libertarian principles, but Pankaj never challenges them. Pankaj''s reply doesn''t say government should ignore those principles. It says the ban isn''t the kind of thing they forbid. Arguing that a rule doesn''t apply is different from rejecting the rule.'),
('c40a32c3-0ce4-4b22-a830-6fc26f568985'::uuid,'10641cbc02edd7d3be850b794f6751e9','Ana AGREES, by assumption. Ana opposes the ban by citing a principle about things "that harm only themselves." That principle only reaches the ban if the ban is aimed at harm to smokers alone.

Pankaj DISAGREES. The ban applies only in public places, and people "could still smoke all they want in private." A ban meant to protect smokers from themselves would leave a strange gap there. A ban meant to protect other people from smoke fits it exactly.

When the second speaker doesn''t touch any explicit claim, look for the assumption that holds the first speaker''s argument together. That''s often where the disagreement lives.'),
('c9c7d3fd-6a1d-4bbb-a8c5-5d180dd7bb40'::uuid,'bbb2757ce41d19e745544ad3d9304b96','Pankaj clearly agrees, saying the ban "would apply only to smoking in public places." Can we put Ana on the other side, believing the ban would not prohibit smoking in public places? No. Ana says nothing about where the ban applies and may well assume it covers public places. Pankaj''s point rests on the word "only," and this answer leaves it out.'),
('b9cbdb41-4d2c-47ee-a0db-3a1c53b6085c'::uuid,'673463a72988de2f8ac3f28d288dc79f','Soltan AGREES: Soltan''s conclusion is that Klein "should step down." Bell DISAGREES: Klein "is just the person we need making important decisions in the future." Someone who should keep making the big decisions shouldn''t step down.

Notice how much the two speakers share: the policies worked, and important decisions are coming. They split only on what to do about Klein. When speakers agree on the facts, look for the disagreement in their conclusions.'),
('248a8c74-1ec6-40b3-93ae-a1e217b4b75e'::uuid,'5382124857e813efaac224975686264d','"Always" makes this too strong to pin on Bernard, whose argument is about Carl''s case, not every view in every situation. And even if Bernard held the general rule, Ayla could accept it too. Ayla doesn''t think the forum would legitimize Carl''s views at all; Ayla thinks it would expose them. The dispute is about what the forum would do, not about whether legitimizing bad views is acceptable.'),
('6d3a9470-f5b7-4dae-9568-a74e25ebcdfe'::uuid,'d2873dfaa9d8d918899b6988471362bc','"Every" makes this too strong for Ayla. Ayla says this forum will provide convincing arguments against Carl''s views and people will see them for what they are. That''s one case, not a claim about every dangerous idea. And Bernard never says what happens when people hear forceful counterarguments. Without clear positions on both sides, this isn''t the disagreement.'),
('b068e848-36fb-4c3c-b345-001bd0e9d697'::uuid,'2d532659124e790c9d1a865f16d81cd2','Ayla AGREES almost word for word: "The best way to combat false views is to challenge them in public." Challenging views in public means letting them be expressed in public. Bernard DISAGREES: giving Carl''s views a platform would "legitimize them" and "help to promulgate them," which is the opposite of fighting them.

The move: when two speakers share a goal (nobody wants Carl''s views adopted), look for the disagreement in the means. Then match the answer''s general words to the case. "Erroneous ideas" stands in for Carl''s false views, and "allowing the public expression" stands in for inviting Carl to the forum.'),
('69dafa2e-e158-440c-baeb-409d650dd4ec'::uuid,'b8846b73545540c45710fc2593de2628','Bernard calls Carl''s views "false, and worse, dangerous," but that''s about Carl''s views, not a general claim that people with false views tend to endanger society. And Ayla never says whether false views make their holders dangerous. Without a clear position from either speaker, this can''t be the point at issue.'),
('e766b047-89e1-46ff-aa38-4a6214ed3601'::uuid,'71c7ef49a1326595b99b498a4f2e99c7','Both speakers seem to agree, so it''s not a disagreement. Bernard wants to avoid anything that would support the adoption of Carl''s views. Ayla wants to combat those views so people "see them for what they are." Neither wants Carl''s views adopted. Ayla never calls them "dangerous," but nothing Ayla says supports encouraging their adoption either.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;